PRE-TABLE CHECK -- #509 sgimtedepth4dead -- written 2026-09-07 BEFORE table 1 runs.
Its mtime is the order discriminator; a block written after a table is a rationalisation.

Detector: regress/sgi_mte_fill_probe.py, 13 rows.
Fix:      dev_sgi_re.c do_mte_transfer(), `if (depth == 4)' -> `if (depth == 32)'.
Measured: 9/12 pre-fix and 12/12 post-fix at 12 rows; 13/13 post-fix after `copy 4th'.
          (The 12-row PRE-fix number is re-measured in the clone as M1 below, because the
          build/ binary is now FIXED and a score with no surviving artifact is the frozen-
          number class.  The 6-row pre-fix run is preserved as witness_prefix_HEAD.raw, 5/6.)


1. SIBLING PARITY
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The edited comparison sits in a block served by THREE case labels -- MTE_TLB_A, _B, _C --
which share one body.  At a block serving several labels the siblings are the LABELS.

    label      rowed?   disposition
    MTE_TLB_A  YES      every row uses it
    MTE_TLB_B  NO       DISCLOSED, not owed.  See the reason below.
    MTE_TLB_C  NO       DISCLOSED, not owed.

REASON, and it is the grok seat's, adopted over the codex seat's contrary reading: the
repaired comparison does not read `dst_tlb'.  The three labels differ only in which TLB
array the helper indexes, which is PACKING, not the comparison under test.  A per-label row
would pin the helper's array selection -- a different property, already the subject of other
rows in this tree.  Codex called B/C "uncovered", which is true and is not the same as owed.
DISCLOSED as coverage debt: a mutant conditioning the repair on `dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A'
escapes this detector.

The OTHER dominating guards in the function -- MTE_MODE_STIPPLE and `src > MTE_TLB_C' --
both terminate the process, so they admit no reachable cell and are correctly unrowed.
The `dst_y_step' guard's exit(1) is commented out, so it does not reject; it is not read in
the tiled arm.  Neither is this round's business.


2. CLAIM -> ROW  (every added comment sentence asserting an observable consequence)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The #509 comment makes four assertions.  Three are rowed; the fourth is a DISCLAIMER and is
deliberately rowed in the negative direction.

  "depth is a count of BITS ... 8/16/32/64 only"
        -> depth8 top + depth16 top + fill top.  Three of the four values are driven.
           Field value 3 (depth 64) is NOT driven -- see section 5.
  "both assignments were dead, every tiled transfer took the CI arm"
        -> fill top (0x44 pre-fix, 0x11 post-fix) and copy 2nd (host stack pre-fix).
  "at 32 bits CI assigns buf[0] alone while memory_rw copies four"
        -> fill bot.  Pre-fix it CANNOT be asserted (the tail is host stack), which is why
           the row is gated and reports n/a rather than scoring a nondeterministic byte.
  "This repairs the 32-bit cell ONLY ... 16-bit and encoding-3 are NOT closed here"
        -> depth16 top pins that 16 still takes CI.  That is the row that keeps the claim
           HONEST: if a later edit silently changed the 16-bit cell, this row reds.
           *** NO ROW ASSERTS THAT THE 16-BIT LEAK IS STILL PRESENT, and none should: the
           leak is unobservable through a defined channel.  The CHANGELOG must therefore not
           claim the leak is closed, and the grok seat's warning is recorded verbatim in the
           round block. ***


3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  guard reads:  depth   (derived solely from mode bits 8-9)
  body writes:  src_mode, dst_mode
  body then reads:  dst_mode  ALWAYS
                    src_mode  ONLY under `mode & MTE_MODE_COPY'

That asymmetry is the whole reason the copy rows exist.  The two written lvalues can
DISAGREE -- one repaired, the other not -- and a fill can never see the disagreement.

    disagreeing state          row that sees it
    dst repaired, src not      copy top (0x4d), copy 4th
    src repaired, dst not      fill top (0x44), copy top (0x00)
    both repaired but to RGB   fill bot (0x00), copy 4th (0x00)

`mode & MTE_MODE_COPY' is a body-tested partition, so the floor cells are
{fill, copy} x {src_mode, dst_mode}.  All four are rowed.


4. CHANNEL -- per row: the mutant it kills, and the observable that differs under it
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  live         no mutant; identity.  Kills a broken harness.  Channel: RAM round-trip.
  devsig       no mutant; identity.  Kills reading RAM instead of CRIME.  Channel: a value
               RAM cannot produce (0x1e000000).
  paint ok     no mutant; identity.  Kills a paint that lands somewhere other than the
               oracle address.  Channel: dest[0] before any transfer.
  fill alive   M4, M6, M7 indirectly.  Channel: process survival.
  fill ran     any mutant that makes the transfer silent.  Channel: dest[0] is a byte the
               fill can produce at all.
  fill top     M1 (verbatim reinstatement), M2, M4.  Channel: dest[0], 0x44 vs 0x11.
  fill bot     M5 (dst RGB-for-RGBA).  Channel: dest[3], 0x44 vs 0x00.
               *** THIS ROW IS THE ONE THE PANEL ADDED.  Without it M5 scores 12/12. ***
  depth8 top   M7 (`!= 8' / always-true).  Channel: dest[0] at 8bpp, 0x44 vs 0x11.
  depth16 top  M6 (`>= 16').  Channel: dest[0] at 16bpp, 0x44 vs 0x11.
               *** ALSO PANEL-ADDED.  depth8 cannot see M6: the branch is cold at 8. ***
  copy alive   M-any that makes the copy path terminate.  Channel: process survival.
  copy top     M2 (dst not repaired -> 0x4d), M3 (src not repaired -> 0x00).
               Channel: dest[0].  Green on HEAD AND post-fix by construction -- it pins
               COUPLING, not the fix, and must not be reported as a witness row.
  copy 2nd     M3, and any mutant leaving the copy path on CI.  Channel: dest[1].
  copy 4th     M8 (src RGB-for-RGBA), M5, M3.  Channel: dest[3], 0x4d vs 0x00 vs 0x1a.
               *** ADDED IN THIS BLOCK, before table 1, because the enumeration showed M8
               escaping all twelve earlier rows.  That is what this check is for. ***

NO ROW HAS A CHANNEL THAT CANNOT VARY UNDER ITS NAMED MUTANT.  The two survival rows are
the closest call: they are identity rows, not mutant discriminators, and are labelled so.


5. DISCLOSED -- cells this detector does NOT cover, stated before the table rather than after
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * MTE_TLB_B and MTE_TLB_C (section 1).  A repair conditioned on the label escapes.
  * Field value 3 / depth 64.  The header defines no MTE_DEPTH_64; driving it would assert
    behaviour nobody has a warrant for.  Two seats corrected my arithmetic here and the
    corrected figure is in the round block: CI leaves SEVEN bytes uninitialised there and
    an RGBA substitution would leave FOUR -- so F2 (`depth >= 32') is a reduction, not a
    fix, and is rejected for want of a semantic warrant rather than because it is neutral.
  * The LINEAR arm (MTE_TLB_LIN_A/B).  It terminates the process on COPY and on any depth
    other than 8, so a depth-32 linear row is host death, not a red row.
  * The `dst_tlb' default arm (values 3, 6, 7): diagnoses and continues.  Not this round.
  * The uninitialised-buffer class at 16 bits and at encoding 3 -- [[sgirebufuninit]].
    *** F1 fills buf[0..3] at depth 32, so it ACCIDENTALLY MASKS that sibling's 32-bit
    tiled cell.  A later round that rows only depth 32 on this path will look fixed and
    will not be.  Keep that sibling alive on depth 16. ***  (grok, pass 1.)
  * [[sgirebufshiftub]] -- every GET arm computes buf[0] << 24 on a uint8_t, which promotes
    to signed int and is UB for a first byte >= 0x80.  Re-derived independently by codex.
    This detector must not stand on it: SRC_WORD's first byte is 0x1a, deliberately.


6. MUTANTS TO BE BUILT, WITH PREDICTIONS MADE BEFORE THE RUN
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Predictions are recorded here so the table can refute them.  A prediction copied into a
measured column afterwards is the frozen-number class in a new coat.

    M1  depth == 32 -> depth == 4          clause (b), verbatim.  predict 10/13
    M2  delete dst_mode |= RGBA            clause (d).            predict  9/13
    M3  delete src_mode |= RGBA            clause (d), the fill-blind one.  predict 11/13
    M4  depth == 32 && (mode & COPY)       clause (d).            predict 10/13
    M5  dst_mode |= RGB (not RGBA)         value substitution.    predict 11/13
    M6  depth >= 16                        over-reach.            predict 12/13
    M7  depth != 8                         over-reach.            predict 12/13
    M8  src_mode |= RGB (not RGBA)         value substitution.    predict 12/13

M1 doubles as the re-measurement of the pre-fix score, which is why it is built first.


==========================================================================================
TABLE 2 -- written 2026-09-07 AFTER pass 2 and BEFORE table 2 runs.  Same order discipline.
==========================================================================================

WHY A SECOND TABLE: pass 2 found a MEASURED TOTAL ESCAPE (`depth >= 32' scored 13/13 on the
shipped 13-row detector -- M9_ge32.raw).  Three seats reached it independently: glm derived it,
the kimi substitute derived it, and grok derived it AND classified it as clause (d) -- the
guard's own operand with its bound relaxed, `==' -> `>=' on the same constant.  That is an L2
floor breach, repaired in-round by a 14th row.

`depth64 top' drives field value 3 and wants the CI byte 0x44.  It asserts what this round does
NOT change; it takes no position on what 64bpp ought to do, which nothing in the header and no
local manual would warrant.  Note M6 (`>= 16') is a LARGER edit and was already killed; the
SMALLER one escaped -- the accept-row-at-the-near-bound shape, with the far neighbour unpinned.

PREDICTIONS AT 14 ROWS.  Recorded so table 2 can refute them; table 1's predictions were each
one high on M1-M4 and that is written into the record rather than smoothed away.

    BASE            14/14
    M1_reinstate    10/14   fill top, fill bot, copy 2nd, copy 4th   (depth64 cold -> green)
    M2_nodst         9/14   + copy top
    M3_nosrc        11/14   copy top, copy 2nd, copy 4th             (depth64 cold -> green)
    M4_copyonly     10/14   as M1                                    (fill at 64 has no COPY)
    M5_dstrgb       12/14   fill bot, copy 4th                       (depth64 cold -> green)
    M6_ge16         12/14   depth16 top AND depth64 top              <- gains a second red
    M7_ne8          12/14   depth16 top AND depth64 top              <- gains a second red
    M8_srcrgb       13/14   copy 4th
    M9_ge32         13/14   depth64 top ALONE                        <- the L2 repair's own kill

ALSO DISCLOSED BY PASS 2, and NOT rowed (clause (e) coverage debt, with its reason):

  * `if (depth == 32 && !(mode & MTE_MODE_COPY))' -- the dual of M4.  Every fill row stays green,
    `copy top' stays green because dest[0] of a same-type copy is invariant, and the gate OPENS,
    so the two tail rows would score bytes a CI-CI PUT never wrote.  There is no copy-local
    four-byte signal that is not those bytes themselves.  UNPINNED, and it cannot be pinned by
    this instrument.
  * M4's own copy-row reds are a WRONG-REASON KILL: under it the copy path really is RGBA and
    dest[3] would be 0x4d, but the fill is CI so the gate closes and both rows red anyway.  The
    mutant is still killed by `fill top'; the CHANNEL those two rows report under it is vacuous.
  * M4 is therefore clause (e), NOT (d).  DEPENDS-ON enumerates predicates DOMINATING the edited
    line plus lvalues the GUARDED BODY reads; the body is exactly the two `|=' statements and
    does not read MTE_MODE_COPY, which is tested later in the loop.  Filing M4 as (d) would
    import its dual into the floor, and that dual is the unpinnable cell above.
